  "summary": "Dry stone hut with conical roof found in the Puglia region of Italy.",
  "description": "Characterized by its whitewashed walls and distinctive conical roofs made of dry stone without mortar. Developed as peasant housing to be quickly dismantled to avoid property taxes, reflecting the region's limestone geology and agricultural history.",
  "culturalContext": "The trulli of Alberobello (Puglia, UNESCO 1996) — dry-stone cone-roofed huts built without mortar — served tax avoidance (demountable structures evaded building levies) and practical climate, whitewashed cylinders capped by stacked limestone courses topped with whitewash-and-symbol pinnacles: sun wheels, heart piercings, primitive crosses painted by roof-masons. Thousands survive as dwellings, shops and Masseria stays; the conical skyline is Italy's most distinctive vernacular landscape.",
